  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 133 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe high continues to shift eastward on Sunday and will remain\neast of NM on Monday. Scattered to numerous storms are once again\nexpected across western NM each afternoon. PWATs will remain at \nor above one inch, so a chance for heavy rainfall and flash \nflooding will persist. However, storm motions of 10-20 mph toward\nthe northeast should help mitigate the flooding threat, unless\nrepeated rounds of precipitation fall over the same area. Much \nlike Saturday, outflows could push into the Rio Grande Valley in \nthe late afternoon or evening, bringing gusty winds and/or a few \nstorms. Meanwhile, the east will remain hot, with more and more \nlocations likely topping the century mark in the afternoon, \nespecially on Sunday. \n\nBoth ensemble means and AI models suggest a new upper high will\ndevelop near El Paso Tuesday afternoon. This will disrupt the\nmonsoon plume, though enough moisture will remain in place to\nrecycle in the form of isolated to scattered thunderstorms over\nthe high terrain. But overall, storms shouldn't be as robust,\nespecially across western NM, as they were Sat-Mon. The high may\nshift northward, more squarely over NM on Wednesday, but\nuncertainty remains on where it will meander to thereafter. For\nnow, a downtick in thunderstorm coverage is forecast.",
